From 9d399c4e1cc55073a6805fe9e18de0c70d70482d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 18:34:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(intake): patches land in inbox/ and do not survive being
 applied
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0020, superseding 0019. Two files reached public main by being uploaded to
the repository root: 0019-root-hygiene.patch, and `nb` — a build tool belonging to
an entirely different project, which then could not run because it hardcoded a path
to that project's workspace.

Neither was caught. Every test asked whether what we had was correct; none asked
whether something we had not put there had arrived.

.GITIGNORE WAS NEVER GOING TO STOP IT

`/*.patch` has been in that file the whole time. A GitHub web upload commits
through the API, and an ignore rule only prevents an UNTRACKED file being staged by
`git add`. Once a file is tracked the rule is silent. Worth knowing in general:
ignore rules are a convenience for `git add`, not a policy about what may exist in
a repository.

AND IT COMPOUNDS

A file at the root you have also touched locally — even just `chmod +x` — makes
`git pull` abort with "local changes would be overwritten". Everything after that
fails for reasons unrelated to the cause: the patch never arrives, the test run is
on stale code, the commit records a mode change under a message about something
else, and the push is rejected as non-fast-forward. One cause, five symptoms, none
pointing at it.

THE ARRANGEMENT

  inbox/                     tracked so an upload has somewhere to land, holding
                             nothing but its own README so nothing accumulates
  scripts/apply-inbox.sh     apply with git am, fold the removal of the patch file
                             into the SAME commit with --amend, run the tests, and
                             reset hard to the previous HEAD if they fail

One commit per patch, containing the change and nothing else. The instruction does
not survive its own execution. --dry-run says what would apply and changes nothing.

Both paths were run, not reasoned about: a good patch produces one clean commit
with an empty inbox, and a patch that breaks a test rolls HEAD back to where it
started and says so.

FIVE GUARDS

  * ROOT_FILES declares every file allowed at the repository root, with a reason
  * the allowlist may not name a file that is no longer tracked — an allowlist that
    outlives what it describes has stopped being a description and become a wish
  * no .patch may be committed anywhere
  * inbox/ may not still hold a patch
  * the apply script is read, and fails if the removal stops being folded into the
    commit — a patch surviving in history is the problem coming back quietly

Also removes `nb` and 0019-root-hygiene.patch from the tree.

351 tests. tsc clean, build clean.

